          <avActivityHighlights>New snow and wind slabs are to be assessed with care and prudence.</avActivityHighlights>
          <avActivityComment>20 to 30 cm of snow has fallen since yesterday above approximately 1600 m. &lt;br/&gt; New snow and wind slabs can in some cases be released by a single winter sport participant. This applies in particular on very steep slopes. &lt;br/&gt; As a consequence of new snow and wind the wind slabs will increase in size additionally as the day progresses. On wind-loaded slopes and on extremely steep slopes avalanches are possible, in particular medium-sized ones. Above approximately 2300 m they can be triggered in the new snow and wind slab layers and reach large size in some cases. &lt;br/&gt; &lt;br/&gt; The Avalanche Warning Service currently has only a small amount of information that has been collected in the field, so that the avalanche danger should be investigated especially thoroughly in the relevant locality.</avActivityComment>
          <snowpackStructureComment>The sometimes moderate wind has transported the new snow. Faceted weak layers exist in the snowpack in particular in places that are protected from the wind. Below approximately 2000 m from a snow sport perspective, in most cases insufficient snow is lying, especially in the Prealps.</snowpackStructureComment>
        </BulletinMeasurements>
      </bulletinResultsOf>
    </Bulletin>
